I am using following tables for calculating the paid time seconds;

# {{company_order}}

# The base table for the orders.

# {{company_order_try}}

# Contains all information regarding the order and its states. By saying states, I mean, when we have an order, machine tries to bind it with driver multiple times until the driver accept the order for a ride. You can see multiple ids against one (order_id and city_id). You can also check the status of that request in the column State. I am calculating the paid time seconds only, where the state=finished, which means the driver actually finished the ride. For rest of the states where state<>finished, i think it is not necessary to capture because in that case, mostly, the ride was not completed or did not happened.

# {{company_order_try_state_log}}

# Once driver accept the ride, all the statuses related to that ride will be captured in this table. As far as i looked into the data, a successful order have 6 rows against  one (order_try_id and city_id) with the states starting from;

# waiting_driver_confirmation
# driver_accepted
# driver_arrived_to_client
# driving_with_client
# arrived_to_destination
# finished

# For the calculation of paid time seconds, i am using states (driving_with_client & arrived_to_destination) and calculating the seconds accordingly.

In the final query, I have added the city so we can compare it with old ETL easily. I have tested the data for multiple drivers for one period (2022-12-30 ) and the data is matching completely, for a complete city, I have checked against Tallinn for the period 2022-12-30 and we have total difference of 2.6% with the old ETL. Please review, lets have a discussion on it whenever you have time.

